---
import TrainingLayout from "../../layouts/TrainingLayout.astro";
import Snippet from "../../components/training/Snippet.astro";
import LiveDemo from "../../components/training/LiveDemo.astro";
import ApiLink from "../../components/training/ApiLink.astro";
import ChapterLink from "../../components/training/ChapterLink.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/training/Callout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---
<TrainingLayout slug="theming-dark-mode">

  <h2 id="variables">Everything is a CSS variable</h2>
  <p>
    <code>main.css</code> (<ChapterLink slug="installation" />) defines more than 100 variables with the prefix
    <code>--ge-table-</code>: backgrounds and text per area and side (<ChapterLink slug="table-model-areas" />), borders, zebra
    rows, hover, focus, selection, progress bars, tree arrows. A light and a dark set are built in.
  </p>
  <Code lang="css" title="@guiexpert/table/css/table-color-vars.css (excerpt)" code={`html, html[data-theme="light"] {
  --ge-table-bg: #fff;
  --ge-table-header-center-bg: rgba(233, 233, 233, 0.5);
  --ge-table-header-center-text: #000;
  --ge-table-body-center-text: var(--ge-table-header-center-text);
  …
}
html[data-theme="dark"] {
  …
}`} />

  <h2 id="own-theme">Your own look</h2>
  <p>Override the variables in a stylesheet that is loaded <strong>after</strong> <code>main.css</code>:</p>
  <Snippet file="demos/theming.css" region="light" title="src/theme.css" />
  <Snippet file="demos/theming.css" region="dark" />
  <p>
    Here the selectors end with <code>.brand-table</code>, so only this one table is themed – CSS variables are inherited, a class on
    any parent is enough. Drop the class to theme every table of the app.
  </p>

  <h2 id="dark-mode">Light and dark</h2>
  <p>The table follows the attribute <code>data-theme</code> on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>:</p>
  <Snippet file="demos/theming.ts" region="switch" />
  <LiveDemo name="theming" title="Brand theme" height={400}
            caption="“light / dark” switches this whole website – the table switches with it. Untick “brand theme” to compare with the default look." />

  <Callout type="pitfall" title="The body follows the header">
    <p>By default <code>--ge-table-body-center-text</code> is <code>var(--ge-table-header-center-text)</code>. Set a coloured header
      text and the body turns the same colour – override the body text variables, too (as above).</p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout type="tip" title="Find a variable">
    <p>Open the browser dev tools, select a cell and look at the computed styles – or search
      <a href="https://github.com/guiexperttable/mono/blob/main/packages/table/css/table-color-vars.css">table-color-vars.css</a>.
      From code: <ApiLink name="CssVars" />.</p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="rtl">Right-to-left: Arabic, Hebrew, Persian</h2>
  <Callout type="version" title="Since 1.1.98">
    <p>Right-to-left tables need <code>@guiexpert/table</code> 1.1.98 or later.</p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    One option mirrors the whole table: <ApiLink name="TableOptionsIf.direction" /> = <code>"rtl"</code>. The first column is on
    the right, horizontal scrolling starts on the right, fixed columns, the tree indent, header groups, the sort icon and the
    column resize handle (now on the left edge) follow – and so do the arrow keys: → moves to the previous column.
    <code>"auto"</code> takes the direction of the page (<code>&lt;html dir="rtl"&gt;</code>).
  </p>
  <Snippet file="demos/rtl.ts" region="rtl" />
  <LiveDemo name="rtl" title="100,000 orders from right to left" height={400}
            caption="Untick “right-to-left” to compare – the same model and options, only <code>direction</code> changes." />
  <p>
    “Left” and “right” in option names mean <strong>start</strong> and <strong>end</strong>: <code>fixedLeftColumnCount</code> fixes
    columns on the right, the class <code>ge-table-text-align-left</code> aligns text to the start (right) – numbers with
    <code>ge-table-text-align-right</code> move to the left, like in a right-to-left Excel sheet (and the Excel export is one).
    Positions of the API count from the start, too: <ApiLink name="TableApi.scrollToPixel" />, the <code>scrollLeft</code> of
    <ApiLink name="EventListenerIf.onScroll" /> and the stored state.
  </p>
  <Callout type="tip" title="Your own CSS">
    <p>Use logical properties – <code>padding-inline-start</code>, <code>margin-inline-end</code>, <code>text-align: start</code> – and
      your styles follow the direction. Only for right-to-left: <code>.ge-table[dir="rtl"] …</code> (the table sets
      <code>dir</code> on its host).</p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="showcase">More</h2>
  <p>The showcase <a href="/showcase/laf/run/">Look and feel</a> switches between several complete themes,
    <a href="/showcase/rtl/run/">Right-to-left</a> shows Arabic sales data. The
    <a href="/doc/#css-classes">documentation</a> lists all CSS classes and variables.</p>

  <h2 id="next">Next</h2>
  <p>Merged cells: <ChapterLink slug="rowspan-colspan" />.</p>
</TrainingLayout>
